{
  "dom_id": "step-q46424",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $10$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $10,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{10}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{10}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{10}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{10+9}={x}^{19}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $19.$",
